WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.940
- Next is Erica Dent,
a second grade teacher

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.935
at Joaquin Miller Elementary
in Burbank, California.

00:00:05.935 --> 00:00:08.220
Hello. How are you doing today?

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.110
- Good, thank you. Thank you.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.210
- [Kelly] So, what's your question?

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.770
- Well, hi, Kelly. Hi, Dr. Biden.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:14.730
- Hello.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.410
- I absolutely love teaching.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.650
I knew from a very young
age that I wanted to teach

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.030
when I thought passing out papers

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.800
was the coolest thing you could do.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.180
And then, as I grew older,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.420
I knew I wanted to help shape lives.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.610
And it wasn't until my ninth grade year

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.860
that I had my first teacher of color.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.920
So, I know the importance
of representation,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.330
but I am concerned.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.440
I know that I feel,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.922
and I'm pretty sure other
educators in the room feel,

00:00:39.922 --> 00:00:41.940
that teaching is getting harder.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.790
I know not many people are
entering the profession

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.220
and so many teachers are
considering quitting.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.760
What do you think we should
do to be able to keep

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.200
this diverse group of teachers

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.630
and then also help feel supported?

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:58.860
- So, I think, you know, we
are our own best advocates,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.240
I think, for the profession.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.240
We know how much we love
teaching. It's who we are, right?

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.950
And so, I think we are the ones,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.020
we have to be the messengers that go out

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:10.950
and spread the word.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.620
Like, if you're in some
job that you don't like,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.220
come and teach, because
you'll find meaning every day.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.890
You'll find that you can
change someone's life.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.680
And what our administration
is doing, which I love,

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:26.670
is that they are now
paying for apprenticeships.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.230
We're gonna pay you

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.200
until you get your teaching certification.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.090
So now, these teaching apprenticeships

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.070
are one of the things
that we're aiming for.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.096
Plus, we must have more teachers of color.

00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:40.459
(audience applauds)

00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:42.360
- And I know that you said
it was important for you

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.675
to see someone that
maybe looked like you -

00:01:43.675 --> 00:01:44.508
- [Jill] Yeah.

00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:45.810
- When you were, but it's
also important for my kid

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:47.220
to see all different walks of life.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.053
- [Jill] Yeah.

00:01:48.053 --> 00:01:49.470
- Like that's, I think
that's just as important.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.950
Like, representation isn't
just for one type of person.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.350
It's for everyone to see everything.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.361
That's the important part.

00:01:56.361 --> 00:01:58.830
(audience applauds)

00:01:58.830 --> 00:01:59.830
Thank you so much.

